\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:concept}{{1}{2}{\textbf {A physical diffusion model with fast hidden units.} (a)~Pixel oscillators, coupled to their neighbours by springs, interact with a thermal bath at temperature $T$. Hidden Potts units (rounded boxes; the filled dot is the active state) are coupled to all pixels (global unit, purple) or to the pixels of one patch, at a coarse (red) and a fine (yellow) scale. They equilibrate fast and are never controlled directly. (b)~Learning at one noise level. The pixels are clamped to a noised image $\x _t$ and the hidden units settle. Each coupling is updated from quantities at its two ends: the clamp force on its pixel and $S$ sampled states of its hidden unit. No free-running phase is needed. Repeating this over images and noise levels gives the schedule $\bm {\theta }^{*}(t)$. (c)~Generation. Starting from noise, an external controller replays $\bm {\theta }^{*}(\tau -s)$ in reverse time $s$. The bars, in the colours of (a), show when each hidden unit acts: the global unit throughout, the coarse patch units from $s\approx 0.5$, the fine ones from $s\approx 2.8$. A final quench with the bath switched off removes the thermal noise. The images are one generated $32\times 32$ face; the network drawings are schematic. \todo {the frames are from the K64 model with 32 patch states: re-render from the locked model, and add the frame after the quench.}\relax }{figure.caption.1}{}}
